agents — see and control local agentic-coding processes (claude, codex, agy, grok, cursor-agent)

LOOK
  agents                       who needs you: a summary, then Needs you (blocked), To
                               review (done under AGENTS_PARKED_MIN, 60 minutes) and
                               Running, one line for what is parked, one for the system
  agents watch [SECS]          the same, redrawn every SECS (default 2); q quits
  agents ls                    the full table: each session's PID, status, CPU, memory,
                               ports and cwd, its MCP servers and commands; stale
                               processes and hogs when there are any; system status
  agents ps [PID]              everything a session runs: MCP servers, each command
                               (tool shell) with its origin (main or a worktree
                               subagent) and flags (dup, long, hot, wait, stuck), nested
                               sessions; all sessions without PID
  agents stale                 only stale processes: orphaned MCP servers, npx
                               helpers, caffeinate, dev servers (ppid 1)
  agents hogs                  top 5 hogs, any owner: memory past 8 GB RSS first,
                               then CPU: lifetime average >= 50% after 30m,
                               or >= 90% for 0m outside agent trees
  agents mem                   memory and swap as bars, pressure, the memory guard, the
                               ten processes holding the most (footprint, which counts
                               compressed pages RSS misses) on a bar to the guard's
                               stop size, with their session and project, and the
                               last incidents
  agents mem check [-n]        one look by the memory guard, saying what it did
                               (-n: what it would do)
  agents mem guard             the guard's loop, every AGENTS_MEM_POLL (5s) while
                               something needs watching, else AGENTS_MEM_IDLE (20s): warns
                               about a process past AGENTS_MEM_WARN (8 GB, or 60% of the stop size), at each
                               doubling, or growing faster than AGENTS_MEM_RATE (200 MB/s);
                               stops one in an agent tree past AGENTS_MEM_KILL (0:
                               half the RAM), or the largest past the warning while
                               pressure is critical (AGENTS_MEM_GUARD=kill|warn|off).
                               The monitoring pack runs it as a LaunchAgent (macOS)
                               or a systemd user service (Linux)
  agents mem log [N]           the last N (20) incidents (memory.tsv)
  agents mem restart           restart that service (after editing the settings)
  agents thermal               thermal pressure and energy mode (display only)
  agents cool [cool|auto|quiet|max|off]
                               fan control (--pack cooling, Apple Silicon): the fans,
                               the SoC's temperature and who runs them; cool, auto
                               (balanced) and quiet raise them with the temperature
                               from 55, 60 or 70 °C, max runs them flat out, off
                               hands them to macOS
  agents show [--json] [--watch] PID[@START]
                               one process in detail: what and whose it is, its
                               verdict, session (id, model, what it is doing now),
                               where it lives (tmux, Herdr, terminal), CPU, memory,
                               threads, ports, files, children; --watch redraws
  agents pick                  browse it all in fzf: the preview is agents show;
                               enter jumps, ctrl-p peeks, ctrl-d details, ctrl-k kills
  agents jump [--notify] PID[@START]
                               go to where it runs: its Herdr or tmux pane, then the
                               terminal tab that shows it (Ghostty, iTerm, Terminal)
  agents root-of PID           the agent session PID runs under, as PID@START, with
                               the terminal app that shows it, its bundle id and the
                               command that jumps there (notify.sh: a banner's click)
  agents peek PID[@START] [LINES]
                               the last LINES (40) lines of its tmux or Herdr pane
  agents mcp [DIR]             the MCP servers Claude Code and Codex are configured
                               with (all, or those a session in DIR loads): name,
                               scope, copies running now (one per session) and
                               their memory, what starts it, where; processes go
                               by these names (never shows args, env, headers or URLs)
  agents mcp lazy|eager NAME...|--all
                               lazy: put mcp-lazy in front of a stdio server in Claude
                               Code's and Codex's configs, so a session starts it on
                               its first tool call, not with the session; eager: back
  agents json | tsv            machine-readable snapshot (tsv feeds SwiftBar)
  agents bar [status]          the menu bar's refreshes: SwiftBar running the plugin,
                               the last poke, an early refresh pending
  agents bar poke [--settle]   refresh the menu bar now (macOS, SwiftBar running the
                               plugin; else nothing): at most one every 2s, the last
                               of a burst at the window's end; --settle: once more
                               then. Claude Code's hooks, Herdr's events and the
                               menu itself run it; AGENTS_BAR_REFRESH is the backstop
  agents why [--copy] [PID[@START]]
                               why an item is listed, the evidence (owner, age,
                               ports, the file a loop watches and its last line)
                               and a verdict: safe to stop, check first, leave
                               it, informational; --copy puts it on the clipboard.
                               Without a PID: every flagged item, one line each

OTHER MACHINES (over ssh: Herdr's saved machines, and AGENTS_REMOTE_HOSTS)
  agents board [MACHINE|local] every session on every machine in one list, by what
                               you would do about it: Needs you, To review, Working,
                               the parked folded away; the right side follows the
                               cursor (why it waits, what runs under it, the end of
                               its pane). enter goes there, tab changes the lens
                               (Attention, Tree, Machines), ctrl-p lists everything
                               you can do, ? the keys. In Herdr: prefix+space
  agents map [MACHINE|local]   what runs where, in fzf, on every machine: previews
                               and dev servers first (port, project, where each
                               opens), then what was left behind, the sessions (one
                               line each; ctrl-e lists what they run) and the
                               machines. enter opens or goes there, ctrl-k stops
                               what is under the cursor (asked first, on its
                               machine), ctrl-s sweeps a machine (agents reap). In
                               Herdr: prefix+m
  agents ports [--pick] [MACHINE|local]
                               what listens where: each TCP port of yours here and
                               on your machines, its command, cwd and where it opens;
                               --pick: in fzf, enter opens it in your browser
  agents forward open [--no-open] MACHINE PORT|URL
                               bring a port of MACHINE here (loopback to loopback,
                               one ssh -L) and open it in your browser: the same
                               port number when it is free here
  agents forward [ls]          the forwards and each one's state: active, nothing
                               answers, or dead (its ssh is gone)
  agents forward close --all | --dead | LOCALPORT | MACHINE PORT
                               --dead: the dead ones and those nothing answers
  agents push [-m MACHINE] FILE...
                               copy files into MACHINE's ~/.cache/tuidev/drop and
                               print the paths (macOS: copied too), to paste there
  agents open [--no-open] MACHINE /PATH
                               fetch one file (up to 50 MB) and open it here
  agents link [status]         the watcher: with it, a Ctrl-click on a localhost
                               link or a file link in a Herdr pane of another machine
                               opens here (a tunnel on a port from 47000, then your
                               browser; the file, fetched). The monitoring pack runs it as a LaunchAgent
                               (macOS); elsewhere run 'agents link watch'.
                               AGENTS_LINK=0 turns it off
                               The other way: a file dropped onto a Claude Code
                               session elsewhere pastes a path that is only here;
                               its prompt hook asks for it, and you are asked, in a
                               dialog, before anything is sent
  agents link restart          restart the LaunchAgent (after editing the settings)

HERDR'S LABELS (this machine's Herdr; AGENTS_HERDR_NAMES=0 leaves them alone)
  agents herdr [status]        each tab's label, what a sync would make it and whose
                               it is; each workspace's roll-up
  agents herdr sync [--throttle] [--poke] [-n] [-q]
                               name each tab after what runs in it (the kind's mark,
                               then the session's task title or the pane's
                               directory, "+N" for more agents), roll up each
                               workspace's sessions and how many are blocked for
                               the sidebar ($tuidev_agents, $tuidev_need), give
                               agent rows the time a waiting state began
                               ($tuidev_since) and their own task title where
                               the tab's name does not say it ($tuidev_session),
                               and mark them. Only a tab still
                               showing Herdr's number, or the name a sync gave it:
                               one you renamed stays yours. Herdr's events run it
                               (the tuidev.link plugin); --throttle: not within 30s
                               of the last; --poke: an agent's state changed, so
                               read Herdr's agents again and refresh the menu bar
                               first
  agents herdr reset [-n]      put back every tab number a sync replaced and clear
                               what it reported
  agents herdr reset [-n] TAB_ID...
                               hand those tabs back to auto-naming, one you renamed
                               too (agents herdr lists their ids): named now as a
                               sync would, else their number; nothing else changes

FROM YOUR PHONE (herdr-core, the runtime Herdr's phone app talks to; --pack herdr-core)
  agents hc [status]           is herdr-core running, and in your desktop login (on
                               macOS a core the phone started over SSH is not: its
                               agents cannot reach the Keychain)
  agents hc up [--restart] [-q]
                               start it in the desktop login; a core that runs is left
                               alone, and one started over SSH is restarted only with
                               --restart (that ends its terminals). At the Mac, not
                               over SSH. -q: the herdr-core pack's job (at login and
                               every 5 minutes) says a problem once; AGENTS_HC_UP=0
                               turns that job off
  agents hc run [--name NAME] [--] CMD [ARG...]
                               start CMD (claude, codex --no-daemon) in herdr-core, in
                               this directory, and put this terminal on it: the phone
                               lists it, and it outlives the pane. In a Herdr pane the
                               sidebar shows its state. ctrl+\ detaches. It gets the
                               core's environment with this shell's PATH, not
                               the shell's other variables
  agents hc auto CMD [ARG...]  off unless AGENTS_HC_AUTO=1: what claude and codex typed
                               in a Herdr pane run (the herdr-core pack's shell
                               fragment). Hosts an interactive start in herdr-core
                               as run does, with this directory and PATH, when a core
                               already runs (in the desktop login, on macOS); else
                               runs CMD as typed
  agents hc attach TERMINAL    back onto one (herdr-core ls names them)
  agents hc ls                 what the phone lists from this machine: each core
                               terminal (hosted or a mirror, its state, its
                               directory), then how many agent panes of this
                               machine's Herdr it cannot reach, and why. Never
                               starts a core
  agents hc mirror [status]    experimental, off unless AGENTS_HC_MIRROR=1: each agent
                               pane of this machine's Herdr and its mirror in
                               herdr-core, a core terminal attached to the pane, so
                               the phone lists the agents you already run. A pane
                               takes its mirror's size, which the phone app sets;
                               a phone-sized pane is named
  agents hc mirror sync [-n] [-q]
                               start a mirror for each agent pane without one and end
                               those whose pane has no agent left. Herdr's events run
                               it (with agents herdr sync) while herdr-core is up
  agents hc mirror fit [PANE]  give a mirrored pane (default: the focused one) its
                               own size back while the phone still shows it; the
                               phone sees its top left until it sets a size again.
                               Focusing the pane at the desk does it
                               (AGENTS_HC_MIRROR_FIT=0: not)
  agents hc mirror end         end every mirror; the agents stay in their panes
  agents hc mirror off [PANE]  no mirror for this pane (or PANE) from now on: its
                               mirror ends, and syncs leave it out until on, or
                               until the pane is gone
  agents hc mirror on [PANE]   mirror it again

HISTORY (local, names only; AGENTS_HISTORY=0 records nothing)
  agents report [--view VIEW] [--since 24h|7d|30d|today] [--json]
                               where the CPU went: CPU-hours by job (test, build,
                               server, browser, mcp, agent, wait, other), project,
                               session and hour, what was cleaned up, and the top
                               insights from the runs (default 24h). VIEW: runs
                               (repeated and slowest commands), waste (duplicates,
                               leaks, polling, failed re-runs), trends (against the
                               window before; default 7d), agents (by agent type),
                               tokens (by model, CLI, agent type, project, session
                               and origin; cache hits, polling, compactions, Codex
                               plan headroom, and what they would cost at API list
                               prices), context (MCP servers, skills and subagents
                               used and loaded, plugins, what is unused; default
                               7d), denials (tool calls denied, by command, with
                               what to try), waits (how long sessions waited on
                               you: blocked, fresh and parked, hour by hour, and the
                               notifications, by project and kind)
  agents runs [--since 24h|7d|30d|today] [--json]
                               finished commands, newest first: when, how long, CPU,
                               peak memory, exit code, project, session, the agent
                               that started it, dup, and whether agents stopped it
  agents limits [--json]       each CLI's usage limits (Claude Code, Codex,
                               Antigravity): used % per window on a gauge marking the
                               time gone, under or over pace (with the time left at
                               this rate), when it resets, how old the reading is
  agents sample [--scheduled]  record one sample; the monitoring pack's sampler
                               runs --scheduled every minute: a sample unless one
                               ran within 55 s, then (AGENTS_AUTO_REAP=1, no menu
                               running) agents reap --auto

ACT
  agents kill [OPTS] PID...    SIGTERM the whole tree, descendants first, then
                               SIGKILL whatever is left after 3s
  agents reap [OPTS]           kill each stale tree whose verdict (agents why) is
                               safe to stop; the plan shows each verdict and how
                               many "check first" ones it left out
    --stuck                    reap: also stop polling loops flagged stuck (the
                               safe ones)
    --all                      reap: include the "check first" ones too
    --auto                     reap without asking only what is safe to: stale
                               trees that listen on no port, loops stuck on a file
                               quiet for 2 x AGENTS_WAIT_STUCK (with
                               AGENTS_AUTO_REAP=1 the menu or the sampler runs it)
  agents term [OPTS] PID...    SIGTERM one process (for hogs), not its tree
    -n, --dry-run              show what would be signalled
    -y, --yes                  don't ask (required when stdin is not a terminal)
    --confirm-gui              ask with a macOS dialog instead (Cancel is the default)
    --notify                   report the result as a macOS notification
    PID@START                  a PID with its start time (from json/tsv): refused if
                               the pid now names a different process
  agents cwd PID@START         that process's working directory (copy-path puts it
                               on the clipboard, reveal shows it in Finder)
  agents open-term [-n] [--hold] CMD...
                               run 'agents CMD' in a new window of your terminal
                               (AGENTS_TERMINAL); -n prints the command. The one
                               exception: 'tuidev update --ask' runs tuidev

KEEP AWAKE
  agents awake [status]        keep-awake state, and who else holds the Mac awake
  agents awake on [DURATION]   hold system and display awake (30m, 2h, 1h30m);
                               no DURATION = until 'agents awake off'
  agents awake agents          hold while any agent session runs, then release
  agents awake lid DURATION    macOS: also keep awake with the lid closed, for at
                               most AGENTS_LID_MAX (8h). Asks for an admin
                               password each time; a root guard restores lid sleep
                               when DURATION ends, below 20% battery off AC,
                               or when thermal pressure reaches heavy
  agents awake off             release (ends lid mode too: one more password)
  agents awake restore         let a closed lid sleep again (pmset disablesleep 0),
                               e.g. after a reboot killed the guard
    --notify                   report lid mode's results as macOS notifications

CLAUDE CODE HOOKS AND STATUS LINE (shipped in ~/.claude/settings.json; JSON on stdin)
  agents hook subagent-stop    stop the background commands a subagent left
                               running once it handed back its final report
  agents hook session-end      on /clear or /resume: stop the session's polling
                               loops and what its finished subagents left
    -n, --dry-run              print what it would stop (or AGENTS_DRY_RUN=true)
  agents hook wait-start       Notification: a session outside Herdr starts
                               waiting on you (permission or idle prompt)
  agents hook wait-end         its next prompt, tool run or turn end ends it
  Only tasks matched to their own process (its stdout is the task's output
  file) under the claude that runs the hook. AGENTS_HOOK_REAP=0 turns it off;
  AGENTS_HOOK_LOG=1 logs each run to @TMP@/data/hooks.log
  agents statusline            Claude Code's status line (statusLine): records
                               Claude's 5-hour and weekly limits (Pro and Max),
                               then prints AGENTS_STATUSLINE_CMD's line for the
                               same JSON, or a compact one with a context gauge

SETTINGS
  agents config [--edit]       effective settings, and the file they came from;
                               --edit opens the file in your text editor
  agents doctor [--fix [-n]]   is the monitoring pack's runtime working: ps can be
                               read, the sampler's last sample, the memory guard,
                               Claude Code's hooks calling this agents, the data and
                               run dirs writable; --fix restarts the sampler's job
                               and the guard (-n: says what it would run)
  @TMP@/none.conf (KEY=value; AGENTS_CONF picks another file).
  'agents config' prints every key with its value, and the file documents each
  one. The same names in the environment win. NO_COLOR=1 turns colors off.
  Stale patterns: AGENTS_ORPHAN_PATTERNS in @REPO@/lib/agents/config.sh.
  Data: @TMP@/data

SAFETY
  Never signals pid 1, this shell or its parents, another user's process
  (prints the sudo command instead), a system or session process (anything
  under /System/Library, /usr/libexec or /usr/sbin; loginwindow, Finder, Dock,
  WindowServer, your terminal), or a process hosting a shell or an agent below
  it. Refuses to run as root. Re-checks each process's start time before every
  signal. Never marks as stale anything that hosts a session (an agent, a
  shell, tmux/screen/zellij/herdr/pm2), a launchd job or a systemd service.
  Keep-awake uses caffeinate (macOS) or systemd-inhibit (Linux); a closed lid
  still sleeps unless the Mac is on AC with an external display (closed-display
  mode). Only 'awake lid' uses 'pmset disablesleep', after macOS's own admin
  dialog, time-boxed, with a root guard that restores it; no sudo rights are
  stored. 'agents' warns when sleep is disabled with no guard to restore it.
[rc=0]
